Q: Is the MessageId field in the client payload guaranteed to match the MessageId obtained after sending a message on the server? A: <p>No, it's not guaranteed. Firebase doesn't document this as a stable contract anywhere, and that's the key problem. If it's not in the docs, they can change it anytime without warning.</p>
<p>From what I've seen (and what you've noticed too), the client-side <code>fcmMessageId</code>/<code>messageId</code> does tend to match the last segment of the server-side message ID. But &quot;tends to&quot; and &quot;guaranteed&quot; are very different things, especially if you're building critical logic around it.</p>
<p>The risky part is you'd be relying on an implementation detail rather than a documented behavior. Firebase could change the format, add prefixes, or break the mapping in a future SDK update and they wouldn't owe you a migration path because it was never promised.</p>
<p>If you need to reliably correlate server-sent messages with client-received ones, the safer approach is to include your own unique ID in the message data payload when you send it. Something like:</p>
<p>js</p>
<pre><code>{
  data: {
    myMessageId: &quot;your-own-uuid-here&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That way you're in full control of the correlation and you're not depending on FCM internals staying consistent. It's a little extra work but it's the kind of thing that saves you from a nasty surprise six months down the line.</p>

Q: How can I pass basic authentication credentials in API calls for a Flutter mobile application? A: <p>Use:</p>
<pre><code>import 'dart:convert';

var auth = 'Basic ' + base64Encode(utf8.encode('$username:$password'));

Future&lt;Response&gt; callAPI(param) async {
    await dio.post('/api/test',
        data: {'param': param},
        options: Options(headers: &lt;String, String&gt;{'authorization': auth}));
  }
</code></pre>
